Clean-room re-creation (ADR-021)
--------------------------------

This cartridge was re-created from a recorded final-result baseline without
access to the prior implementation or its upstream; it implements the geometry
of the five regular convex polyhedra (the Platonic solids) and the standard
tabletop dice numbering convention.

The slug `polydice` previously held a cartridge whose origin licence was
BSD-2-Clause. Under the operator ruling recorded as ADR-021, any hyperobject
whose origin licence is not CERN-OHL-W-2.0 leaves the commons and returns as a
clean-room re-creation. That cartridge was removed on 2026-09-04 and the slug
reserved.

The implementer of this cartridge did not read, fetch or otherwise consult:

  - the removed cartridge's source, in this repository's history or in the
    archived satellite repository of the same slug;
  - the upstream OpenSCAD project the removed cartridge derived from;
  - any fork or mirror of either.

The only inputs were a baseline pack recording the removed version's *final
result* — its manifest contract (modes, parts, parameters, presets, i18n) and
measured geometry (sizes, circumradii, volumes, body counts) — together with
the public mathematics of the regular polyhedra.

No third-party files are vendored here, and this cartridge depends on no
third-party design. The geometry of a regular tetrahedron, cube, octahedron,
dodecahedron and icosahedron is public mathematics, not anybody's code; the
numbering convention (opposite faces summing to n+1, vertex numbering on the
d4) is long-standing common practice in the tabletop trade.

Lineage recorded in `project.json` → `project.attribution.lineage`:
`regular polyhedra (public geometry)`.

Departure from the recorded baseline
------------------------------------

The recorded baseline was inert: all 80 of its renders were one byte-identical
mesh containing fourteen dice on a 30 mm grid, and no mode or parameter had any
effect. This cartridge implements what the manifest contract promises rather
than what that baseline did. `docs/CLEANROOM-VERIFICATION.md` records the
measurements and states each deviation.
